# Claim a GitHub issue

First step of every work cycle. Use this to take ownership of a GitHub issue, surface any open questions, and post a plan comment so other agents and the user can see what you're about to do.

## Inputs

- An issue number passed as argument (e.g. `/claim 123`)
- Or, if no number given: pick from currently `{{LABEL_READY}}` issues

## Steps

### 1. Identify the issue

If an issue number was passed, use it. Otherwise:

```
gh issue list --label {{LABEL_READY}} --state open
```

If more than one is ready, ask the user which to claim using `AskUserQuestion` with structured options.

### 2. Read the issue thoroughly

```
gh issue view <id> --comments
```

Note:
- Acceptance criteria (explicit or implied)
- Any prior agent's plan or attempted work
- Linked issues / PRs
- Stakeholders mentioned

If the issue body is sparse, pull in the linked design docs or specs before proceeding.

{{IF ADR_LOCATION}}
### 3. Cross-check architecture decisions

ADRs override intuition. Read every Accepted ADR in `{{ADR_LOCATION}}` whose subject overlaps the issue.

```
ls {{ADR_LOCATION}}
```

For each ADR that touches this work:
- Note its decision
- Confirm the issue doesn't conflict
- If it does, cite the ADR and ask the user before continuing

If unsure whether an ADR applies, read it. Skipping ADR cross-check is the most common cause of correct-per-issue / wrong-per-architecture PRs.
{{ENDIF}}

### 4. Ask clarifying questions

If anything is ambiguous, ask before claiming. Use `AskUserQuestion` with structured options. Examples:

- "The issue says 'fix the export'; should it apply to CSV only or all formats?"
- "The acceptance criteria mention 'fast'; what's the target latency?"
- "Two reasonable interpretations of step 3 exist: (a) ... (b) ... — which?"

Don't claim with unresolved ambiguity. Guesses compound; questions get resolved cheaply now and expensively later.

### 5. Apply labels and self-assign

```
gh issue edit <id> {{IF AGENT_LABEL}}--add-label {{AGENT_LABEL}} {{ENDIF}}--add-label {{LABEL_IN_PROGRESS}} --remove-label {{LABEL_READY}} --add-assignee @me
```

This signals to other agents and the project board that the issue is taken.

### 6. Post a plan comment

Draft a 3-5 bullet plan and post it as a comment. The comment survives session restarts and is visible to other agents.

```
gh issue comment <id> --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
## Plan

- Bullet 1: what you'll do first
- Bullet 2: ...
- Bullet 3: ...

Will close: #<id>
EOF
)"
```

Keep the plan honest. If you're not sure how to approach something, say so in the comment.

### 7. Hand off to the next phase

The issue is now claimed and `{{LABEL_IN_PROGRESS}}`. Continue with the project's next phase — usually clarify-and-challenge (Phase 2) or design (Phase 3 — `wovenflow:designflow`).

## Anti-patterns

- **Claiming silently** — others can't see it; risk of duplicate work
{{IF ADR_LOCATION}}- **Skipping the ADR cross-check** — produces PRs that satisfy the issue but violate architecture
{{ENDIF}}- **Claiming with unresolved ambiguity** — every guess will be wrong in at least one dimension
- **No plan comment** — if your session crashes, future-you starts from zero
- **Stale labels** — leave the issue in `{{LABEL_IN_PROGRESS}}` while you sleep on it; never forget to advance it to `{{LABEL_IN_REVIEW}}` when the PR opens
